\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Marine inertial wave-motion estimation is more difficult than attitude
estimation because accelerometer bias, attitude error, and low-frequency model
mismatch integrate into velocity and displacement, whereas the motion
is itself oscillatory.  The difficulty is strongest horizontally, where
tilt-induced gravity leakage competes with real surge and sway, and must be
controlled across sea states with widely varying periods and acceleration
levels.

Recent heave estimators include discrete Kalman reconstruction, adaptive IMU
filtering, nonlinear observers, and spectral approaches
\cite{Reis2023_DiscreteKalmanHeave,Lu2023_AdaptiveDelayFreeHeave,
Li2025_HeaveCKF,YurovskyKudinov2025_IMUwaves,Hope2025_SFY}.  First-order
Gauss--Markov acceleration models, including the Ornstein--Uhlenbeck process itself,
are classical \cite{UhlenbeckOrnstein1930_OU,Singer1970_ManeuverModel}.  This work
embeds such a prior in a fully specified three-dimensional quaternion MEKF with an
integral-displacement state, covariance-consistent regularization, and an
exogenous sea-state adaptation layer whose contribution is isolated by
controlled ablation.

\subsection{Main contributions}
The contributions are:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.25em]
  \item a 21-state left-multiplicative quaternion MEKF coupling attitude,
        gyro and residual accelerometer bias, and three exact OU-driven
        $[v,p,S,a_w]$ chains, with $S$ providing soft low-frequency drift
        control;
  \item a two-level stability result: finite-horizon observability,
        finite-correlation residual-bias contraction, bounded Riccati
        covariance, and UES yield local 21-state Live ISS, while the
        independent Mahony startup observer gives an almost-global,
        semiglobal practical attitude-capture extension into that Live basin
        uniformly on compact startup sets contained in the proxy's desired
        almost-global domain under the explicit handoff conditions of
        Secs.~\ref{app:iss-stability} and \ref{sec:semiglobal-stability};
  \item an exogenous adaptation architecture that estimates wave time scale and
        acceleration level from measurement-only signals and schedules the OU
        prior and integral regularizer.  Sea-similarity arguments determine the
        OU amplitude/time mappings and averaging horizons, while reduced
        physical-MSE optimization and spectral-moment evaluation provide the
        deployed SpectralMSE regularizer law and its analytical default
        coefficient; complete-filter experiments then validate those choices;
  \item a versioned paired evaluation of vertical and horizontal displacement,
        attitude, bias tracking, stationary and changing seas, and adaptation
        channels.  With the current isotropic configuration, OU--III reduces
        paired mean 3-D displacement RMS relative to matched OU--II in all four
        stationary JONSWAP cases and the controlled transition, while ablation
        identifies integral-state regularization as the principal source of
        the adaptive benefit.
\end{itemize}

Here, $\vct p$ denotes bounded wave-induced motion about a local reference, not
geodetic position; GNSS-aided absolute trajectory estimation is not evaluated.
The evidence is primarily synthetic, and the ESP32--S3 exercise establishes
source portability only.  The semiglobal result covers the first quality-gated
proxy-to-Live handoff subject to an explicit Live-basin-entry condition; later
magnetic re-gauging and tilt re-lock remain separate hard reconfiguration maps.
Thermal-calibration performance is not evaluated.